producer_report.summary:
I wrote docs/design/per-job-main-landing.md, which keeps the proposed design (cut each job's commit from current main) but checks the review's premise first. The 1842.1-minute stage is mostly a measurement effect. `landing_end` takes the newest landing record that lists the job. One job's commit appears in 60 of the 215 landing files, from about 4 minutes after its verifier to about 6,900 minutes later. The note does not claim the review's 1830.0 minutes as saved.

The saving is stated in minutes from verifier pass to first landing record. The only measured value is a sample from agent-and-build-slots.md §6.1, with a median of 4.6 minutes. The newest-20 value is not measured here, so the claimed real saving is 0 to about 5 minutes, labeled as an estimate. The note also has:
- an Existing code section placed before the design;
- an alternatives table whose first row is measuring from the first landing record;
- the failure cases: merge conflict, main moved and verified content differing;
- a rule that the next review reports both the unchanged stage and the first-landing stage;
- a rule that an implementing change cites review 20261109-014617-5cg0.

The rest of the change is a pointer in per-job-landing-branches.md that replaces its citation duty, a CHANGELOG entry, and decision D167. The policy and mirrors tests pass; I left the full test run, fmt and clippy to Athena since no Rust file changed.
